REDBOARD — HOW TO USE & WHAT TO EXPECT
========================================
Last updated: June 2026


WHAT IS REDBOARD?
-----------------
Redboard is an adversarial AI review panel. When your team is about to ship an AI feature,
you submit a decision brief. Five specialist agents debate it in parallel — Safety, Business,
Data Quality, Compliance, and Contrarian — and a Synthesizer produces a structured verdict:
SHIP, DO NOT SHIP, or SHIP WITH CONDITIONS.

The agents have access to Google Search so they cite current regulations and recent incidents,
not just training data. Every review produces a branded PDF memo emailed to you within minutes.


WHO SHOULD USE IT
-----------------
- ML engineers about to deploy a model to production
- Product managers making a build vs buy decision involving AI
- Engineering leads doing a pre-mortem on an AI feature
- Compliance teams wanting a structured AI risk assessment
- Anyone who wants a second opinion before a consequential AI decision


THE FOUR-STEP FORM
------------------
Redboard collects information across four steps. The more detail you provide, the more
specific and useful the analysis will be. Generic inputs produce generic outputs.

STEP 1 — The Proposal (required)
  Project Name: A short name used as the PDF filename and email subject.
                e.g. "ClaimsIQ v1.0" or "CustomerBot Launch"

  Proposal:     What are you shipping? Be specific.
                Include: the system name, what it does, who it affects, deployment scope.
                Bad:  "An AI chatbot for customer support"
                Good: "Deploy a RAG-based chatbot to handle tier-1 billing queries for
                       50,000 enterprise users across 12 insurance clients, with auto-
                       escalation to human agents for queries the model scores below 0.7"

  Context:      Everything technical. The agents read this most carefully.
                Include: model architecture, training data (size, source, date range),
                evaluation metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1), test set composition,
                deployment environment, latency requirements, known limitations,
                any prior A/B testing or shadow mode results.

  Objectives:   What does success look like in specific numbers?
                e.g. "Reduce claim processing time from 8 days to 24 hours.
                Cut cost per claim by 35%. Process 10,000 claims/day at launch."

STEP 2 — Company Context (optional but recommended)
  Team:         Who built this and what is their background?
                e.g. "3 ML engineers, 1 data engineer, 2 backend engineers, 1 PM.
                No dedicated compliance or legal review done yet."

  Timeline:     When does it need to launch and why?
                Any deadline pressure is important — agents will probe this.
                e.g. "Soft launch in 3 weeks. Board presentation July 15 hard deadline.
                Sales has pre-sold 3 clients contingent on July launch."

  User Base:    How many users, what type, what are the stakes?
                e.g. "50,000 MAU across 12 enterprise clients. B2B."

  Domain:       Industry vertical.
                e.g. "Insurance — auto and homeowner claims"

  Geographies:  Where will it operate? This affects regulatory analysis significantly.
                e.g. "US only at launch. Two clients have EU policyholders."

  User Type:    B2B enterprise, B2C consumers, internal employees, etc.

  Tech Stack:   All major components.
                e.g. "GPT-4o via Azure OpenAI, LangChain, XGBoost, AWS ECS, PostgreSQL"

  Stakeholders: Everyone affected — end users, regulators, internal teams, partners.

STEP 3 — Risk & Governance (optional but recommended)
  Known Risks:      Risks the team has already identified and how they plan to mitigate them.
                    Do not hide risks — agents probe harder when risks are understated.

  Regulatory:       Applicable regulations.
                    e.g. "HIPAA, Colorado SB 169, California AB 2013, EU AI Act High-Risk"

  Data Sensitivity: Select from the dropdown — agents weight compliance analysis accordingly.

  Human Oversight:  What is the human in the loop?
                    e.g. "All REJECT decisions require human adjuster confirmation.
                    Auto-approve only for claims under $5,000 with confidence above 95%."

  Rollback Plan:    How quickly can you disable this if something goes wrong?
                    e.g. "Feature flag per client. Shadow mode available. 2-hour rollback."

  Success Metrics:  What will you monitor post-launch?
                    e.g. "Daily accuracy vs override rate, fraud detection rate, latency p99"

STEP 4 — Documents (optional but strongly recommended)
  Upload any supporting documents. Agents read every file and cite specific content.
  The more you provide, the more specific the analysis.

  Most useful file types:
  - Eval reports (CSV, TXT) — agents will analyse your metrics and find gaps
  - Architecture docs (TXT, MD) — agents will find risks the team normalised
  - Model cards — agents will challenge evaluation methodology
  - Code snippets (PY, JS) — agents will identify security and logic issues
  - Slide decks as text — agents will challenge business assumptions

  File limits: PDF, TXT, MD, PY, JS, JSON, CSV, YAML — max 10MB each


SKIP THE FORM — UPLOAD A COMPLETE BRIEF
----------------------------------------
If you have a single document that describes your project, use the "Skip the form" option
at the top of the New Review page. Upload one file (PDF, TXT, or MD) and click submit.
The agents will extract all context from the document automatically.

For best results, your document should include:
  - What the system does and who it affects
  - Model architecture and training data details
  - Evaluation metrics and how they were measured
  - Team size and background
  - Timeline and any deadline pressure
  - Known risks and mitigation plans
  - Tech stack
  - Stakeholders and regulatory environment
  - Deployment plan and rollback strategy

See sample_brief.txt for an example of a complete brief.


