WHAT TO EXPECT FROM THE PANEL
------------------------------
TIMING
  The panel takes 30 to 90 seconds depending on brief length and document volume.
  All five agents run in parallel — it does not take 5x longer for 5 agents.
  Do not close the browser tab while it runs.

THE RESULTS SCREEN
  Verdict banner:     SHIP (green) / DO NOT SHIP (red) / SHIP WITH CONDITIONS (orange)
  Confidence level:   HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW — how certain the panel is
  Risk score:         X/10 — overall risk level (10 = extreme, 1 = trivial)
  Risk summary table: One-line assessment from each of the five agents with their risk level
  Key findings:       The 4-5 most important cross-cutting insights from all agents
  Conditions:         Specific, testable requirements if verdict is SHIP WITH CONDITIONS
  Minority dissent:   Verbatim quote from the harshest agent if they disagree with the verdict
  Next steps:         Numbered, actionable recommendations with suggested owners
  Agent reports:      Full individual analysis from each agent — click tabs to switch

WHAT GOOD OUTPUT LOOKS LIKE
  - Findings reference specific details from YOUR brief, not generic AI advice
  - Document findings cite [Doc: filename] with specific content
  - Conditions are specific ("obtain HIPAA BAA with all clients before processing claims")
    not generic ("improve compliance processes")
  - The contrarian agent asks a question that reframes the entire decision
  - The synthesizer weighs severity relative to the nature of the system —
    an internal advisory tool is not held to the same standard as a consumer
    automated decision system

WHAT THE AGENTS WILL FIND (that teams often miss)
  - Biased test sets (training and test data from the same pool)
  - Auto-approval or auto-rejection paths with no human fallback
  - Regulatory gaps the team assumed did not apply
  - Timeline pressure driving premature launch decisions
  - Single points of failure in the operational model
  - Specific numbers in eval reports that undermine the headline accuracy claim
  - Engineering concerns buried in architecture documents that the team normalised
  - The question nobody on the team is asking out loud

EMAIL DELIVERY
  When the review completes, three files are emailed to your registered address:
  1. Decision Memo_datetime.pdf  — the full adversarial analysis (all agent reports)
  2. Submission Brief_datetime.pdf — all your form fields formatted as a document
  3. Submission Details_datetime.zip — brief PDF + all uploaded source files

  Check your spam folder if the email does not arrive within 2 minutes.
  Gmail users: check the Promotions tab.

PDF DOWNLOAD
  The PDF is also available directly from the results screen and from Review History.
  Filename format: ProjectName_YYYYMMDD_HHMM.pdf


TIPS FOR BETTER ANALYSIS
-------------------------
1. More context = better analysis
   The agents cannot find risks in information you did not provide.
   A three-line proposal gets a generic review. A detailed brief gets a specific one.

2. Upload documents whenever you have them
   An eval report CSV is worth more than describing the metrics in text.
   The agents read file contents directly and cite specific lines.

3. Do not sanitise your risks section
   Teams that hide known risks get more aggressive analysis, not less.
   Agents probe harder when the risk section is suspiciously clean.
   Disclosing a risk upfront with a mitigation plan is the right move.

4. Include deadline pressure if it exists
   "Board presentation July 15 requires production metrics" is exactly the kind
   of context that changes an analysis. The contrarian agent in particular
   will surface whether a timeline is technically driven or commercially driven.

5. Use the project name field carefully
   It becomes the PDF filename and email subject. Use something specific:
   "ClaimsIQ v1.0" not "AI Project" or "Test"

6. Review history stores everything
   Every review you run is saved and downloadable from the Review History page.
   You can re-download the PDF at any time.


